[2026-09-07T15:35:00Z] [CHALLENGER] completed F4-T2 -> /src/flight_server_challenger.py. Independent MarketDataFlightServer variant implemented from spec §10 only (src/flight_server.py NOT read). Structural differences from the spec reference (which F4-T1 executor follows closely): (1) Dict-based dispatch table — class-level _ACTION_HANDLERS registry maps action-type strings to bound handler methods (_handle_initial_load/_handle_daily_load/_handle_on_request_load/_handle_daily_report), do_action looks up the handler and serialises its returned dict to flight.Result vs spec's if/elif chain that builds flight.Result inline in each branch. (2) Regex-based SQL validation — _validate_select_only uses a compiled _SELECT_ONLY_RE (anchored at start, case-insensitive, tolerates leading whitespace and -- line comments before the SELECT/WITH keyword) plus a defense-in-depth _FORBIDDEN_RE scan that rejects any embedded DML/DDL keyword (INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE/DROP/ALTER/CREATE/TRUNCATE/GRANT/REVOKE/ATTACH/DETACH/COPY/PRAGMA/VACUUM/EXPORT/IMPORT/CALL) — vs spec's strip+upper+startswith prefix check. (3) Custom exception hierarchy — FlightQueryError/FlightActionError decouple internal failures from transport-level flight.FlightServerError; a single _wrap_flight_error middleware converts them at the do_get boundary vs spec's three separate except clauses. (4) Result-construction helpers — _build_load_result/_build_report_result/_derive_status centralise JSON payload assembly and the status-derivation rule (SUCCESS if no tickers_failed, PARTIAL if failures, SKIPPED passthrough) so each handler stays tiny and the rule lives in one place vs spec's inline status computation repeated in each branch. (5) Handlers return plain dicts and never touch flight.Result directly — the dispatcher serialises — making handlers trivially unit-testable without Flight types. Same public API as spec §10: module-level _validate_select_only(sql)->None and _truncate_sql(sql, max_len=200)->str; MarketDataFlig